  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how wind power technology is evolving from turbine hardware to grid integration and policy. On the left, offshore wind turbine support structures are compared, with side and top-down views showing several foundation types in shallow water, such as multi-leg, monopile, jacket-like, and platform-style layouts. Below that, turbine scale is emphasized by a size comparison: a conventional 2.5 MW turbine is much smaller than newer models, including a 15 MW Vestas machine and an even larger 50 MW concept, with landmarks like the Statue of Liberty and the Eiffel Tower included for visual reference.\n\nThe center-right panels shift from individual turbines to power-system roles. One sketch suggests wind turbines interacting dynamically with the grid rather than simply delivering power one way. Another shows wind turbines paired with energy storage to provide broader system services, including grid-forming capability. A further diagram indicates AI-based coordination or control linking multiple turbines to the grid.\n\nThe rightmost panel highlights policy support, specifically EU rules for high-voltage direct-current connections. Overall, the image conveys a progression toward larger turbines, more sophisticated offshore infrastructure, smarter control, storage integration, and regulatory frameworks that enable wind plants to behave more like active grid-supporting assets.",
